    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-221769",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$(n+1)^{2}>0$ for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n(n+1)^{2}{{field-4}}0.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+1)^{2} & =({{field-6}}+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & {{field-8}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "worked_solution": "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,(n+1)^{2}>0.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,(n+1)^{2}>0)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,(n+1)^{2}\\le 0\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that \n\n$$\n(n+1)^{2}\\le 0.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=-1$ suffices:\n\nBy substituting $n=-1$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+1)^{2} & =(-1+1)^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=-1$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
